# Angelic Orchestrator

## Honest limits — read this before anything else
"Triggers the right plan the right way at all times" is the name Dr. Rahman asked for, not a claim this file can back. No skill can guarantee a right outcome every time — a guarantee like that is exactly the kind of invented-certainty number his own `verify.md` bans ("no scores, no decimals, no vibes"). What this skill actually does: it forces every task through the same five gates below before any sub-agent moves, so a wrong plan gets caught by structure instead of by luck. That is a real improvement in hit rate. It is not omniscience, and it says so out loud rather than quietly overclaiming.

## 0. Hub gate — read before dispatch, touch nothing
Read `hub.md`, `goal.md`, and `verify.md`''s standing rubric before dispatching anything. These three are fixed for the task''s duration. Fast-twitch and slow-loop both write *down* into them; neither edits them from above mid-task. If the task doesn''t fit the live `goal.md`, stop and say so before spending a single sub-agent call.

## 1. Mode select — fast-twitch or slow-loop
Ask one question: is there already a lead that has confirmed more than once?
- **Yes → fast-twitch.** Take the non-contiguous jump (1→5→7 — skip what the lead skips), the way a diagnostician follows a chain of reasoning rather than checking every intermediate box. If the next checkpoint also confirms, escalate: allocate more sub-agents and resources to that lead, never fewer. Once triggered, it runs to completion — it is a long committed path, not a shortcut answer.
- **No, or this is standing/background work → slow-loop.** Gather → reason → verify → write-back, on repeat. Nothing in the spine is a closed, one-shot process; the slow loop never reports itself "done," only "current state as of this pass."

Each mode draws on its own corpus — fast-twitch''s is not slow-loop''s — and both are vectorized and embedded separately.

## 2. Privacy-zone tag — before any sub-agent touches real material
Tag the task with one of the five zones (Public / Private work / Confidential / Privileged / Restricted) before dispatch. Apex litigation material, medical or journal material, and anything under an existing `*_PRIVILEGED` folder defaults to Confidential-or-higher and stays local — no public RAG, no upload to a shared index — until Dr. Rahman personally downgrades the tag.

## 3. Sub-agent write-back contract (the Apex-skeleton pattern)
A sub-agent never hands back raw material. Every write-back carries, for each fact: its strategic use, its weakness, and its next action. A write-back with the fact but none of the three gets bounced back to the sub-agent — it does not get forwarded to the hub.

## 4. Separate-verifier gate
Whoever built it does not grade it. A second pass, fresh eyes, grades pass/fail per rubric line only — no invented confidence percentages, no p-value without a stated null hypothesis, no self-graded "100% accuracy." Any output carrying a statistics-sounding number (a %, a confidence score, an accuracy claim) must show its derivation, or the verifier fails that line by default.

## 5. Stop budget (inherited from the Level-5 metaprompt — not relaxed here)
2 rounds per task. 3 consecutive same-line verifier fails → halt and surface to Dr. Rahman. 10 actions with no forward progress → halt. Compact at ~70% context. These exist so a bad plan stops cheaply instead of running to an expensive, confident-sounding completion.

## 6. Telemetry — every session and every day, not just per task
Write one `telemetry.md`-format line at the end of every session, and at least once per day even mid-task:
`YYYY-MM-DD · task-slug · outcome:PASS/FAIL · rounds:n/2 · breakers:none/named · detours:n(what,why) · handoffs:n · note:one line`

## 7. Output contract (his own, carried forward, not new)
Answer first, no preamble. Structure shown, not described. ≤120 words of prose around any artifact. Clinical analogy before any technical term. Confidence stated as High/Medium/Low plus one line of why. One next step, named — never a menu.


ARGUMENTS: /spine /mega-loop — full session export (thinking, inputs, outputs, deltas, artifacts) to PDF + artifact + JSON + markdown + SQL in one folder, for third-party analysis and faithful re-creation');
INSERT INTO blocks (block_id, turn_uuid, turn_no, seq, role, kind, chars, tool_name, tool_use_id, is_error, redacted, signature_sha256, signature_len, privacy_zone, zone_reason, zone_match, text) VALUES (245, '7ce4ffa3-56d1-431a-a6af-9df3197e50e6', 185, 2, 'assistant', 'tool_use', 821, 'Bash', 'toolu_01AaqYqrYDxWgXasAQJioHWX', 0, 0, NULL, NULL, 'Private work', 'session-internal', '', '{"command": "T=/root/.claude/projects/-home-user-claude-/7f4839a0-2759-5274-a271-16ea4d44156c.jsonl; ls -la \"$T\" 2>&1; echo \"--- lines ---\"; wc -l \"$T\" 2>/dev/null; echo \"--- top-level keys / types ---\"; python3 -c \"\nimport json,collections\np=''$T''\ntypes=collections.Counter(); keys=collections.Counter(); roles=collections.Counter()\nn=0\nfor line in open(p,encoding=''utf-8''):\n    line=line.strip()\n    if not line: continue\n    n+=1\n    try: d=json.loads(line)\n    except Exception: continue\n    types[d.get(''type'')]+=1\n    for k in d: keys[k]+=1\n    m=d.get(''message'') or {}\n    if isinstance(m,dict): roles[m.get(''role'')]+=1\nprint(''records'',n)\nprint(''types'',dict(types))\nprint(''roles'',dict(roles))\nprint(''keys'',dict(keys))\n\" 2>&1 | head -20", "description": "Inspect the session transcript"}');
